A triviality, one might say, and easy to understand. But there are other
factors as well that are able to easily fool ones subjective feeling about
available quantities. Too bad if those feelings disable judgement for what
is likely or possible. The random generation probability numbers tells us
half of the story, the observed and experienced variances the other half.

Daggers, e.g.; the greater amount of available daggers is mostly because
you get them from the standard equipment of many common monsters, not
necessarily because they are randomly lying around or created as death
drop. Those random generation effects are often outweighed by other random
sources; I gave monster equipment as example, but also shops. A single
5x10 square shop will significantly influence the amount of respective
objects available in the dungeons. If you don't have such shops generated
in your dungeon you have to rely on what the RNG gives you. One person who
will often see one shop every other level will have a different subjective
impression about object availability in the dungeons than someone who had
a couple games without even one shop. In the latter case the person would
miss a lot objects completely and will have less than average objects of
the other types.

